{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "02a-conditions-in-code.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Simplifying conditions in code",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Read an `if` chain as a formula, find its smallest form, find the branches that can never run, and check that a rewrite changed the code's shape and not what it does.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "An application of lesson 2, outside the course's order: nothing later depends on it. The last section uses Lean, which lesson 3 introduces.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "```ts\nfunction canEdit(isAdmin: boolean, isOwner: boolean, locked: boolean): boolean {\n  if (isAdmin) return true;\n  else if (isOwner && !locked) return true;\n  else if (isOwner && locked && isAdmin) return true;\n  return false;\n}\n```",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Name the conditions $a$ (`isAdmin`), $o$ (`isOwner`) and $l$ (`locked`). A later branch runs only when the earlier ones failed, but every branch returns `true`, and $A \\lor (\\lnot A \\land B) \\equiv A \\lor B$: so the function is true exactly when one of the branches' conditions is.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "truthtable(a ∨ (o ∧ ¬l) ∨ (o ∧ l ∧ a))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The smallest DNF",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Five true rows, so the DNF read off the table has five terms. `minimize` finds the *smallest* DNF with the same table, by the **Quine–McCluskey** method. It has one idea, used over and over, and then a choice at the end. Here it is by hand first.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "**The true rows, a term each.** A row is a term that is true in that row only: $a$, $o$, $l$ true is $a \\land o \\land l$; $a$, $o$ true and $l$ false is $a \\land o \\land \\lnot l$. The five true rows:\n\n1. $a \\land o \\land l$\n2. $a \\land o \\land \\lnot l$\n3. $a \\land \\lnot o \\land l$\n4. $a \\land \\lnot o \\land \\lnot l$\n5. $\\lnot a \\land o \\land \\lnot l$",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Merging two terms\n> Two terms **merge** when they have the same variables and differ in one of them only, plain in one and negated in the other: $a \\land o \\land l$ and $a \\land o \\land \\lnot l$. Together they say \"$a \\land o$, and $l$ either way\", which is $a \\land o$:\n> $$(x \\land l) \\lor (x \\land \\lnot l) = x \\land (l \\lor \\lnot l) = x.$$\n> So the pair can be replaced by the shorter term, without the variable they differ in.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "**Round 1** tries every pair of the five. Five pairs differ in one variable only:\n\n- 1 and 2 differ only in $l$: they merge into $a \\land o$.\n- 1 and 3 differ only in $o$: $a \\land l$.\n- 2 and 4 differ only in $o$: $a \\land \\lnot l$.\n- 2 and 5 differ only in $a$: $o \\land \\lnot l$.\n- 3 and 4 differ only in $l$: $a \\land \\lnot o$.\n\nThe other pairs differ in two variables or more (1 and 4 differ in $o$ and in $l$) and do not merge. Every one of the five merged with something, and a term that merged is not needed any more: $a \\land o \\land l$ is true only where $a \\land o$ is, so the shorter term says all it said. What is left is five shorter terms: $a \\land o$, $a \\land l$, $a \\land \\lnot l$, $o \\land \\lnot l$, $a \\land \\lnot o$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "**Round 2** does the same with those five. Only terms with the same variables can merge, so $a \\land o$ can merge with $a \\land \\lnot o$, and $a \\land l$ with $a \\land \\lnot l$:\n\n- $a \\land o$ and $a \\land \\lnot o$ differ only in $o$: they merge into $a$.\n- $a \\land l$ and $a \\land \\lnot l$ differ only in $l$: they merge into $a$ too, the same term, kept once.\n- $o \\land \\lnot l$ has nothing to merge with: $a \\land \\lnot l$ has the variable $a$, which it has not. It stays as it is.\n\n**Round 3** has $a$ and $o \\land \\lnot l$, which do not merge. The method stops.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Prime implicants\n> The terms that merge no further, here $a$ and $o \\land \\lnot l$, are the **prime implicants**. Each is true only in true rows (it came from them), and none can lose a variable and stay so. Every smallest DNF is made of prime implicants.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "**The cover.** The DNF must still be true in each of the five true rows, so the last part picks prime implicants until every true row has one that is true in it. A prime implicant that is the *only* one true in some row is **essential**: no DNF can leave it out. Here both are essential:\n\n- $a$ is the only one true in row 1, $a \\land o \\land l$ (there $l$ is true, so $o \\land \\lnot l$ is false);\n- $o \\land \\lnot l$ is the only one true in row 5, $\\lnot a \\land o \\land \\lnot l$ (there $a$ is false).\n\nSo the smallest DNF is $a \\lor (o \\land \\lnot l)$. When there are more prime implicants than needed, some are left out; the feature-flag exercise below has one that is.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The engine shows the same work. Each round's merges are steps under it, a pair each (click a step for its explanation in full), and the cover's terms are steps under it, each with the row it is kept for:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "minimize(a ∨ (o ∧ ¬l) ∨ (o ∧ l ∧ a))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The third branch was **dead code**: whenever $o \\land l \\land a$ holds, $a$ already returned. The function is\n\n```ts\nconst canEdit = (isAdmin: boolean, isOwner: boolean, locked: boolean) => isAdmin || (isOwner && !locked);\n```\n\nand `equiv` checks the rewrite against the original, every row of it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "equiv(a ∨ (o ∧ ¬l) ∨ (o ∧ l ∧ a), a ∨ (o ∧ ¬l))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A rewrite that goes one step too far is caught with the row that shows it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "equiv(a ∨ (o ∧ ¬l) ∨ (o ∧ l ∧ a), a ∨ o)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> The smallest formula is not always the clearest code. Simplifying shows what the condition *is*; whether to write it that way is a question of what the next reader needs. The check is worth having either way: it says the two are the same, or exactly where they are not.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Branches that never run",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "```ts\nfunction shipping(express: boolean, member: boolean, heavy: boolean): string {\n  if (express && !heavy) return \"air\";\n  if (heavy) return \"freight\";\n  if (member || !express) return \"ground\";\n  return \"standard\";\n}\n```",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Each `return` runs when its condition holds *and every earlier one failed*. With $e$, $m$, $h$ for the three flags, `\"ground\"` runs when $\\lnot(e \\land \\lnot h) \\land \\lnot h \\land (m \\lor \\lnot e)$. Its smallest form:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "minimize(¬(e ∧ ¬h) ∧ ¬h ∧ (m ∨ ¬e))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\lnot e \\land \\lnot h$: `member` does not matter at all. And `\"standard\"` runs when all three conditions fail. If that has no satisfying assignment, the line never runs:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sat(¬(e ∧ ¬h) ∧ ¬h ∧ ¬(m ∨ ¬e))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Conditions that depend on each other",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So far each condition was a flag of its own: `isAdmin`, `isOwner` and `locked` can be true or false in any combination, so every row of the table can happen. Conditions in code are often about the same value, and then some rows cannot happen. `minimize` can use that, if it is told.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "```ts\nfunction freeShipping(items: number, member: boolean): boolean {\n  if (items > 5 && member) return true;               // a big order\n  if (items > 3 && items <= 5 && member) return true; // a medium one\n  return false;\n}\n```",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Name the conditions: $\\mathit{g5}$ for `items > 5`, $\\mathit{g3}$ for `items > 3`, and $m$ for `member` (typed `g5`, `g3` and `m`). `items <= 5` is the opposite of `items > 5`, so it is $\\lnot \\mathit{g5}$. The function returns `true` when $(\\mathit{g5} \\land m) \\lor (\\mathit{g3} \\land \\lnot \\mathit{g5} \\land m)$. To the formula, $\\mathit{g5}$ and $\\mathit{g3}$ are two unrelated variables, so it has all 8 rows, and the smallest DNF over all 8 still has two terms:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "minimize((g5 ∧ m) ∨ (g3 ∧ ¬g5 ∧ m))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "But a number above 5 is above 3. A row where $\\mathit{g5}$ is true and $\\mathit{g3}$ false, `items > 5` but not `items > 3`, is a row of the table that no value of `items` reaches.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Don't-cares\n> A row that cannot happen is a **don't-care**: the code never meets it, so a simpler condition may be true or false there, whichever makes it smaller. `minimize(φ, d)` takes a second formula $d$, true exactly in the rows that cannot happen. Here $d$ is $\\mathit{g5} \\land \\lnot \\mathit{g3}$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "minimize((g5 ∧ m) ∨ (g3 ∧ ¬g5 ∧ m), g5 ∧ ¬g3)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The don't-care rows join the true rows as terms, so they can merge; one of them, $\\mathit{g5} \\land \\lnot \\mathit{g3} \\land m$, lets $\\mathit{g3} \\land m$ cover what $\\mathit{g5} \\land m$ covered, and $\\mathit{g5} \\land m$ is not needed. Read back, the two branches were one:\n\n```ts\nconst freeShipping = (items: number, member: boolean) => items > 3 && member;\n```\n\nThis rewrite is right only because of what the don't-care says, which the formula alone does not know. `equiv` compares all 8 rows, and finds the two differ in the row that cannot happen:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "equiv((g5 ∧ m) ∨ (g3 ∧ ¬g5 ∧ m), g3 ∧ m)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "On the rows that can happen, those where $d$ is false, they agree:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "taut(¬(g5 ∧ ¬g3) → (((g5 ∧ m) ∨ (g3 ∧ ¬g5 ∧ m)) ↔ (g3 ∧ m)))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Answer with a formula, typing `&&`, `||` and `!` or the glyphs. For `minimize`, the answer must be a DNF with the same truth table and no bigger than the smallest; ↻ Another one gives another.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "minimize((b ∧ ¬t) ∨ (b ∧ t ∧ s) ∨ (¬b ∧ s ∧ t))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "A feature is on when `(beta && !trial) || (beta && trial && signedIn) || (!beta && signedIn && trial)`. With $b$, $t$, $s$, find the smallest DNF.",
      "hints": [
        "Two of the terms share $t \\land s$ and differ only in $b$.",
        "$b \\land \\lnot t$ is the only term true when $b$ holds and $t$ does not."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "practice": "logic.minimize",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "equiv((p ∧ ¬q) ∨ (p ∧ q) ∨ (¬p ∧ q), p ∨ q)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Someone rewrote `(p && !q) || (p && q) || (!p && q)` as `p || q`. Is the rewrite right? Answer true or false.",
      "hints": [
        "The three terms are three of the four rows. Which row is missing, and what does `p || q` say there?"
      ],
      "practice": "logic.equiv",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sat(¬(a ∧ b) ∧ ¬a ∧ ¬(¬b))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "In `if (a && b) return 1; if (a) return 2; if (!b) return 3; return 4;` can `return 4` run? Give an assignment that reaches it, or `false` if none does.",
      "hints": [
        "It runs when all three conditions fail: $\\lnot(a \\land b) \\land \\lnot a \\land \\lnot\\lnot b$.",
        "$\\lnot a$ and $b$: then $a \\land b$ fails too."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Checking it in Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "With the conditions as `Bool`s, a function is a truth table Lean can run, and `decide` checks a statement about every row by running it. Lesson 3 introduces Lean; here it needs only one tactic.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def canEdit (isAdmin isOwner locked : Bool) : Bool :=\n  if isAdmin then true\n  else if isOwner && !locked then true\n  else if isOwner && locked && isAdmin then true\n  else false\n\ndef canEdit' (isAdmin isOwner locked : Bool) : Bool :=\n  isAdmin || (isOwner && !locked)",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem canEdit_simpler : ∀ a o l, canEdit a o l = canEdit' a o l := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that the rewrite does what the original does, for every input.",
      "leanSolution": "  decide",
      "hints": [
        "Three `Bool`s are 8 rows: `decide` evaluates both sides in each."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A wrong rewrite does not get a proof. `decide` says the statement is false instead:\n\n```\ntheorem wrong : ∀ a o l, canEdit a o l = (a || o) := by decide\n-- error: Tactic decide proved that the proposition\n--   ∀ (a o l : Bool), canEdit a o l = (a || o)\n-- is false\n```",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "inductive Ship where\n  | air | freight | ground | standard\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\ndef shipping (express member heavy : Bool) : Ship :=\n  if express && !heavy then .air\n  else if heavy then .freight\n  else if member || !express then .ground\n  else .standard",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem shipping_never_standard : ∀ e m h, shipping e m h ≠ .standard := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that `shipping` never answers `standard`: the last branch is dead.",
      "leanSolution": "  decide",
      "hints": [
        "The same tactic: a statement about finitely many inputs, each checked."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem shipping_ignores_member : ∀ e m h, shipping e m h = shipping e false h := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that `member` makes no difference.",
      "leanSolution": "  decide",
      "hints": [
        "Compare each input with the same one where `member` is `false`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lean goes further for a `match`: an arm that can never be reached is an error, not a warning.\n\n```\ndef route (admin owner : Bool) : String :=\n  match admin, owner with\n  | true, _      => \"full\"\n  | false, true  => \"own\"\n  | false, false => \"none\"\n  | true, true   => \"never\"\n-- error: Redundant alternative: Any expression matching\n--   true, true\n-- will match one of the preceding alternatives\n```",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> An `if` chain whose branches return the same thing is the $\\lor$ of their conditions; a later branch also has the earlier ones fail. `minimize` finds the smallest DNF (with don't-cares for rows that cannot happen), `sat` finds whether a branch can run at all, and `equiv` checks a rewrite, naming the row where it goes wrong. In Lean, `decide` proves the same things about `Bool` functions by running them on every input.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
